# clang-tidy ratchet baseline
#
# Measured by D00 T01 §3 on 2026-09-17. Ratcheted by D07 T01 §2, which is what
# makes it one-way: a count above this fails, equal passes, below rewrites this
# file down in the same commit.
#
# Format: the first line that is not blank and does not start with '#' is
# `count: <integer>`. Everything else here is for the person reading it.

count: 52

# ── How to reproduce this number exactly ─────────────────────
#
# A baseline nobody can re-derive is a number, not a measurement. Three things
# decide it, and all three have to match:
#
# 1. THE TRANSLATION UNITS. Every entry in `build/release/compile_commands.json`
#    whose file is not under `_deps/` and does not end in `.rc`. That was 13.
#    Taking the list from the compile database rather than from a glob is what
#    keeps it honest: a source nobody compiles cannot hide findings.
#
# 2. THE CONFIG. The repository's `.clang-tidy`, which is why the check set is
#    committed rather than passed on a command line.
#
#      clang-tidy -p build/release --quiet <file>
#
# 3. THE DEDUPLICATION. Count UNIQUE (file, line, column, check) tuples, with
#    the file path normalised: separators to '/', '..' segments collapsed, and
#    case folded. Without this the number is wrong twice over. A diagnostic in
#    a header is reported once per translation unit that includes it, so the
#    raw line count was 75 against 52 real findings; and the same header is
#    reached as both `resolute/theme.h` and `resolute/controls/../theme.h`,
#    which counts twice unless the path is normalised.
#
# Release, not debug: release is what ships, and only release carries the
# optimiser-dependent analyser paths.

# ── What the 52 are ──────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   25  performance-no-int-to-ptr
#   12  bugprone-switch-missing-default-case
#    8  performance-enum-size
#    2  performance-unnecessary-value-param
#    1  bugprone-use-after-move
#    1  misc-redundant-expression
#    1  bugprone-misplaced-widening-cast
#    1  bugprone-implicit-widening-of-multiplication-result
#    1  bugprone-nondeterministic-pointer-iteration-order
#
# The 25 `performance-no-int-to-ptr` are Win32 talking: LPARAM and WPARAM are
# integers that carry pointers, and the cast is the API rather than a mistake.
# They are the first candidates for a targeted suppression, not for a fix.
#
# ── The one that is a defect rather than a finding ───────────
#
# `bugprone-use-after-move` at shared/resolute-ui/src/animation.cpp:55
#
#   uint32_t AnimationManager::Add(Animation anim) {
#       anim.id = m_nextId++;
#       m_animations.push_back(std::move(anim));
#       return anim.id;                          // <-- read after the move
#   }
#
# This returns the right value today, and only by accident. `Animation` has an
# implicit move constructor, so its `uint32_t id` is copied rather than stolen
# and survives the move. The day `Animation` gains a user-defined move, or `id`
# stops being trivially copyable, this starts returning garbage to every caller
# that stores an animation handle, and it will not look like a memory bug.
#
# Left in the baseline deliberately: D00 T01 §3 owns the gate, not the code the
# gate found, and fixing it here would be the section widening itself. It is
# called out so the ratchet in D07 T01 §2 starts with a known first target
# rather than a flat list.
